## Session heartbeat marker

A `UserPromptSubmit` hook (`~/.claude/hooks/session-heartbeat.sh`) injects a
ground-truth turn number and timestamp before each of your replies, as a
`session-heartbeat: turn #N at <timestamp>` context line. Copy those exact
values — never invent your own — and end every reply with a line in this
exact form:

`⏱ turn #N · <timestamp>`

This exists so the user can tell, from the outside, whether a reply was
generated with this turn's injected context intact. If you ever notice one
of your own recent replies is missing this line, say so explicitly — that
signals context or earlier instructions were lost mid-session, and the user
may want to restart the session rather than continue.

## Never print live secrets

A `PreToolUse` hook (`~/.claude/hooks/deny-secrets.sh`) blocks Bash commands
and Read-tool calls that would pull a protected secrets file into context, or
that would echo/cat/grep out an `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`/`ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN`
value or a file containing one — including `pm2 logs <app>`, which it
pre-resolves via `pm2 jlist` and content-scans before allowing. That hook
covers Bash and Read — it does not cover typing a key into your own reply,
or any other tool that might surface file contents (an MCP tool fetching a
config file, `docker logs`, `kubectl logs`, `curl`, `journalctl`, or any
other command whose output isn't predictable ahead of time). Treat this as
your own rule everywhere the hook can't reach:

- Never paste a live API key/token value into a chat reply, even partially
  or "for reference" — not even when quoting a config file back to the user.
- If you need to check whether a secret is present in a file, check for its
  *presence* (e.g. `grep -c PATTERN file`, or note "key is set: yes") rather
  than printing the match itself.
- If a key is ever exposed (echoed, catted, pasted into a reply), tell the
  user it should be treated as compromised and rotated — regardless of
  whether the hook caught it, since terminal/session history retains it
  either way.

A `PostToolUse` hook (`~/.claude/hooks/warn-secrets-posttooluse.sh`) catches
what `PreToolUse` can't predict: it scans every completed Bash tool result
for a live-secret-shaped string and, if found, feeds you a `SECRET EXPOSURE
DETECTED` message via stderr (exit code 2). It cannot undo the exposure —
the tool already ran and the output is already in your context — so when
you see that message, treat it exactly like noticing the leak yourself:
tell the user immediately that the key must be rotated, and do not repeat
the full value again in your own reply.

## Never edit another registered project's files directly

A `PreToolUse` hook (`~/.claude/hooks/deny-cross-project-edit.sh`) blocks
Edit/Write/NotebookEdit calls, and write-shaped Bash commands (redirects,
`sed -i`, `cp`/`mv`/`rm`, `git commit`/`push`/etc., opening an editor, ...),
whose target path resolves inside a *different* project than the one this
session is running in — "different project" meaning a directory (or an
ancestor of the target path) with its own `.claude/settings.json` whose
`project.slug` differs from this session's own. If you get denied by this
hook: do not retry with a different command to route around it. Instead,
treat it the same as any cross-project request you decide on your own
initiative — send a question/notice or file a task through whatever
cross-session channel this repo uses (e.g. `message_send`/
`memory_upsert(kind="task", filed_from_project=...)` if this is a
memory-bank-mcp project), or, if no such channel applies here, stop and ask
the user directly before touching that project's files.
